  "body": "I was looking into fixing https://www.w3.org/Bugs/Public/show_bug.cgi?id=23516 and got confused.\r\n\r\n> The CanvasRenderingContext2D 2D rendering context represents a flat linear Cartesian surface whose origin (0,0) is at the top left corner, with the coordinate space having x values increasing when going right, and y values increasing when going down. The x-coordinate of the right-most edge is equal to the width of the rendering context's output bitmap in CSS pixels; similarly, the y-coordinate of the bottom-most edge is equal to the height of the rendering context's output bitmap in CSS pixels.\r\n\r\nThis sentence is no longer accurate, I think. In particular you can have a 200x200 output bitmap, but render it on 100x100 CSS pixels. I thought that was the idea anyway when we did away with high definition backends. (https://github.com/whatwg/html/commit/285b52f213855dffbc6185ead6f26baa9ce9059f appears to be the commit where this language was introduced by the way, although it's as part of a large set of other changes so it's not super clear what's going on.)\r\n\r\nIt's not entirely clear to me how much language needs to be updated for this. @junov thoughts?\r\n\r\nThen:\r\n\r\n> On high-definition displays, for instance, the user agent may internally use bitmaps with two device pixels per unit in the coordinate space, so that the rendering remains at high quality throughout.\r\n\r\nThat should be four device pixels, no?\r\n\r\nAnd the last thing I noticed is that \"over-sampling\" should be \"oversampling\".",
